am I pleased to say the shadow attorney  general Emily Thornbury joins us once  again and stood up very good morning  um can we start with that just did the  inflation figures out today when Jeremy  Hunt says you know his priority is  bringing down inflation my mind suggests  a link between uh what is happening  today and of course the the strike  action the the request from various  parts of the public sector for  significant increases to their pay I  think it makes it less likely that the  chancellor will be able to provide them  with money what is your view well I  think my view is is that um this is  welcome that the inflation rate is going  down but it's still the second worst  month that we've seen for 40 years  um you know inflation of over 10 percent  means obviously that people's wages are  being eaten into  um and and as we've heard you know food  prices are much higher than that so what  needs to happen is that we need to have  proper negotiation so I know I was I was  on your program a month ago I think you  know when there was the dispute  happening in relation to just the  railways at that point and I said my  concern was that the government  could be tempted to play games with this  and could be tempted to try and play it  to their political Advantage instead of  actually looking for a solution sure  sure what they should be doing but  living people around a round a table  yeah it should have been labor would no  more agree to what the rmt or indeed the  the Royal College of nurses are asking  than the conservatives would you know  that we would not be arriving at the  last minute if for example in the rmt  strike and be talking about about  driver-only trains you know at the last  minute when it looked like then maybe  there was a there was a solution finally  being found we would not be calling in  the RCN and saying to them do talk to us  nurses but let's not talk about pay I  mean we would not be doing that we would  be negotiating and we would be trying to  find a solution because a strike is a  failure a strike is a failure on both  sides both sides need to compromise and  you need people to sit around a table  and work out what the compromise is do  you detect that the public support for  for the strikes which on which Dr has  generally been you know in terms of the  public set and indeed in the railway  workers do you detect that that public  support is starting to to Wing slightly  no I don't I think that people see that  there needs to be a solution and I think  you know I think people are greatly  inconvenienced and and that makes people  upset and angry and disappointed that  they're not able to do what they hope so  I think that there's still there seems  to be you know support and let's let's  not forget we're not just talking about  the public sector I mean there are many  strikes in the privacy of course of  course when I was talking to one of the  bosses from mcvitty's where there was a  strike that was settled there was hardly  any publicity about it but the both the  employer and the unions took a pragmatic  View and they sorted it out but when it  comes to to the Health Service and very  specifically the nurses it is it is less  inconvenience and it is more than actual  damage may be done this morning we have  you know NHS England's cancer director  warning that urgent cancer surgery could  be canceled no no that is surely an  outcome that is not just unwanted it is  intolerable of course and and again  listening to what the nurses are saying  they're saying actually this is out of  date there were agreements this week in  the end both the employers and the  nurses want the same thing when it comes  to cancer patients emergency cases they  want to make sure that people are looked  after so it is just a question of of  sorting it out you know and and nurses  will not you turn away from somebody  who's a cancer patient or an emergency I  mean nobody believes that a nurse would  do that what they do want is they want  to have a decent pay settlement and they  want to have if they want to they want  to have more staff they want to not be  so overstretched they want to be  appreciated I wonder what you make if we  can turn our attention to immigration  that which we heard from from Rishi Suna  yesterday A Deal apparently being done  with with Albania I mean just just as a  point of principle if people are not  supposed to be here and they are  unsuccessful in an asylum claim labor  doesn't have a problem with them being  sent back to where they came from as  quickly as possible no what we think is  we should have an immigration system and  an asylum system which is fast and fair  yeah and firm we need to be able to get  decisions made about people and then if  they are refused they are refused and  they should be returned that's always  been our policy  Albania plan well  well I don't think you can have it have  a blanket you're from Albania therefore  you're going home because you know we  know that we need to look at individual  cases because you can't assume that this  woman has not been trafficked if she  says that she has you need to look at  that so you can't have blanket policies  but what you do need to do is you need  do you start making some decisions and  that's what they're not doing and then  you hear from the Prime Minister oh  we're going to make everything's going  to be fine we're going to double the  number of people in the backlog by the  end of next oh yeah and then you ask  what the backlog is and they're talking  about the backloggers just being before  June this year you think that's right  probably well so I mean think about it  right the backlog is just from June last  year right the bits before that so we're  going to have from June and then they're  going to take a year to clear it they  say so let's be generous and say that's  true that would mean by the end of next  year will be 18 months behind anyway do  you see I do I mean it's like it's all  you have to do when they when they say  these things is go well that sounds good  how's that going to work and it falls to  pieces can I ask you to pop your  lawyer's hat on for a second okay of  course the the story they did has been  discussing this morning the the idea  that the UK is now helping Ukrainian  judges train up for for dealing with the  well the war crimes that have been  taking place within their borders I mean  what specifically will they be being  obviously first and foremost do you  appreciate the plan but what is it that  you think that these judges can gain  from this yeah so I think it's great I  think it's really important  um and uh and and you know this  initiative has our full support I've  been I've been out to the Hague and I've  been in discussions about this there's a  number of things that can be done so  first of all there are cases that can be  done in terms of absolutely at the  Grassroots so if a soldier shoots a  civilian for no reason they should be  arrested and they should be tried  straight away and the and and the  additional training that's being given  to judges in Ukraine is great because  those cases need to absolutely stand up  but then you need to go further up the  chain and you need to look at the  generals and you need to look at those  responsible and you need in fact to be  able to reach right up to Putin because  Putin is in the end the person who sent  them in and he's the one who has agreed  to these bombing campaigns against  civilians and the whole aggression is  his responsibility so it is a question  of of making sure that the International  Community comes up with solutions that  will work together in order to hold the  Russians to account for the appalling  things they've been doing in Ukraine  Emily Thornbury great Italian in the  studio thanks very much

